Angle 1 — Check for raw distributed traces (spans) on SCCM hosts

Services are normally built from spans — so even if the service entity wasn't created, raw span data might still exist and could be queried directly.

fetch spans, from:-24h
| filter isNotNull(dt.entity.process_group_instance)
| lookup [
    fetch dt.entity.process_group_instance
    | fieldsAdd h = runs_on[dt.entity.host]
    | lookup [
        fetch dt.entity.host
        | filter arrayIndexOf(tags, "APMID:APP00005497") != -1
        | fields id
      ], sourceField:h, lookupField:id, prefix:"hh_"
    | filter isNotNull(hh_id)
    | fields id
  ], sourceField:dt.entity.process_group_instance, lookupField:id, prefix:"proc_"
| filter isNotNull(proc_id)
| fields span.name, duration, request.is_failed
| limit 20

Angle 2 — Check for a Process Group (not instance) level service association

Sometimes the service link exists at the process_group level rather than the specific instance:

fetch dt.entity.process_group
| filter contains(entity.name, "w3wp") or contains(entity.name, "sitecomp") or contains(entity.name, "sms")
| fields id, pg_name = entity.name

Angle 3 — Check for IIS-specific extension metrics (a different namespace entirely)

IIS has its own dedicated metric set separate from generic service metrics — worth checking if this exists instead:

fetch metric.series
| filter contains(metric.key, "iis") or contains(metric.key, "aspnet") or contains(metric.key, "w3wp")
| summarize count(), by:{metric.key}
| sort metric.key asc

Angle 4 — Check network connection/session metrics (a proxy for request activity)

We found dt.process.network.sessions.reset_local earlier in your own metric catalog — this and related session metrics won't give response time, but could approximate connection volume/health as a rough proxy:

fetch metric.series
| filter contains(metric.key, "network.sessions") or contains(metric.key, "network.connections")
| summarize count(), by:{metric.key}
| sort metric.key asc

Angle 5 — Check if IIS logs are being ingested (W3C log format has response time/status built in)

If OneAgent or a log-forwarder is shipping IIS logs, request-level data (URL, status code, response time) would be sitting right there in log format, queryable without any service entity at all:

fetch logs, from:-24h
| filter contains(log.source, "iis") or contains(log.source, "w3wp") or contains(log.source, "u_ex")
| lookup [
    fetch dt.entity.host
    | filter arrayIndexOf(tags, "APMID:APP00005497") != -1
    | fields id
  ], sourceField:dt.entity.host, lookupField:id, prefix:"hh_"
| filter isNotNull(hh_id)
| fields content, log.source
| limit 20
